-- [Viral Life Cycle: Lytic and Lysogenic] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher grows a pure culture of Escherichia coli that carries an integrated λ prophage. Cells are dividing normally and no free phage can be detected in the medium. The researcher then exposes the culture to mitomycin C, a chemical that damages bacterial DNA. Over the next hour, the optical density of the culture (a measure of intact cell number) falls sharply, and free phage particles appear in the medium. Which statement best accounts for this result?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The DNA damage induced the prophage to excise and switch to the lytic cycle, lysing the cells and releasing new phage"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The DNA damage caused the prophage to integrate more stably, and the cells lysed from the chemical stress alone"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The DNA damage triggered the cells to transfer the prophage to neighbors by conjugation, releasing phage without lysis"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The DNA damage permanently removed the prophage, curing the cells so they kept dividing without making phage"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).'' A temperate bacteriophage such as λ can replicate its genome passively as an integrated prophage during the lysogenic cycle, but an environmental stressor can flip the switch: the prophage excises from the host chromosome and enters the lytic cycle, producing new virions that burst the cell. The textbook notes that in the lysogenic cycle the viral genome is incorporated as a prophage and passed to daughter cells, and that "Environmental stressors such as starvation or exposure to toxic chemicals may cause the prophage to excise and enter the lytic cycle." (Choice A) is correct: mitomycin C is a DNA-damaging chemical stressor, so it induces the prophage to excise and enter the lytic cycle; lytic replication lyses the bacteria (the falling optical density) and releases free progeny phage into the medium (the appearance of phage particles). (Choice B) is incorrect: induction does the opposite of stabilizing integration—the prophage leaves the chromosome—and the appearance of new phage particles cannot be explained by chemical toxicity acting on the cells alone, since a chemical does not manufacture virions. (Choice C) is incorrect: conjugation is a bacterium-to-bacterium transfer of DNA through a pilus, not a mechanism for releasing free phage, and it would not cause the culture-wide lysis reflected in the sharp drop in optical density. (Choice D) is incorrect: DNA damage induces the lysogenic-to-lytic switch rather than silently deleting the prophage, and a "cured" culture that kept dividing would show rising optical density and no free phage, the opposite of what was observed. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of prophage induction to predict how a lysogenic culture responds to a DNA-damaging stressor, using the drop in cell number and appearance of free phage as evidence.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '103107f8-e563-4981-af97-07f5623229fd';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '103107f8-e563-4981-af97-07f5623229fd';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('103107f8-e563-4981-af97-07f5623229fd', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'stress deepens dormancy rather than triggering induction'),
  ('103107f8-e563-4981-af97-07f5623229fd', 'C', 'misconception', 'confuses phage release with bacterial conjugation'),
  ('103107f8-e563-4981-af97-07f5623229fd', 'D', 'misconception', 'believes stress cures the lysogen instead of inducing lysis');

-- [Bacterial Growth Curve] ans=D medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A microbiologist inoculates fresh broth with 5,000 E. coli cells that are already dividing by binary fission at a steady rate, with a doubling time of 20 minutes. Assuming this rate holds and no cells die, approximately how many cells will the culture contain after 2 hours?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Approximately 10,000 cells"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Approximately 30,000 cells"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Approximately 60,000 cells"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Approximately 320,000 cells"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'D',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).'' Prokaryotes such as bacteria reproduce by binary fission, in which one cell divides into two genetically identical daughter cells; because every cell in the population divides each generation, the number of cells grows exponentially rather than by a fixed amount. The population is described by \(N = N_0 \times 2^n\), where \(N_0\) is the starting number and \(n\) is the number of generations (doublings) that have occurred. Over 2 hours (120 minutes) with a 20-minute doubling time, \(n = 120/20 = 6\) generations, so \(N = 5000 \times 2^6 = 5000 \times 64 = 320{,}000\). (Choice A) is incorrect: multiplying the starting count by 2 only once (\(5000 \times 2 = 10{,}000\)) treats the entire 2-hour interval as a single doubling, ignoring that the cells double repeatedly, once every 20 minutes. (Choice B) is incorrect: multiplying the starting count by the number of generations (\(5000 \times 6 = 30{,}000\)) applies linear growth, but binary fission compounds the population, so growth is exponential (\(2^n\)), not additive/proportional to \(n\). (Choice C) is incorrect: multiplying by \(2 \times 6 = 12\) (\(5000 \times 12 = 60{,}000\)) mistakenly reads "double 6 times" as "multiply by 12"; six successive doublings multiply the count by \(2^6 = 64\), not by 12. (Choice D) is correct: six doublings in 2 hours multiply the initial 5,000 cells by \(2^6 = 64\), giving roughly 320,000 cells. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the exponential growth relationship \(N = N_0 \times 2^n\) for binary fission, first inferring the number of generations from the elapsed time and doubling time and then compounding the population rather than adding to it.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'feaf7bef-bb73-4e3d-837f-e8e7a07f37b0';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'feaf7bef-bb73-4e3d-837f-e8e7a07f37b0';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('feaf7bef-bb73-4e3d-837f-e8e7a07f37b0', 'A', 'misconception', 'single doubling instead of compounding'),
  ('feaf7bef-bb73-4e3d-837f-e8e7a07f37b0', 'B', 'misconception', 'linear growth instead of exponential'),
  ('feaf7bef-bb73-4e3d-837f-e8e7a07f37b0', 'C', 'misconception', 'misreads doublings as a multiplier (2×n)');

-- [Prokaryotic Cell Wall & Gram Staining] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher uses an enzyme to fully digest away the peptidoglycan wall of a rod-shaped freshwater bacterium, leaving an intact plasma membrane and cytoplasm. The bacterium''s cytoplasm has a high concentration of dissolved solutes, and its freshwater medium is dilute. The wall-less cell is then returned to that same freshwater medium. What is the most likely outcome?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The cell swells and bursts as water enters and the membrane expands past its limit."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The cell shrinks and crenates as water leaves the concentrated cytoplasm."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The cell keeps its rod shape and volume because the membrane alone blocks water."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The cell loses its shape but survives because internal pressure holds it steady."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).'' The bacterial cell wall is a protective layer outside the plasma membrane whose functions include giving the cell shape and rigidity and preventing osmotic lysis. Because "the cytoplasm of prokaryotic cells has a high concentration of dissolved solutes," the osmotic pressure inside the cell is high, so water tends to flow inward when the cell sits in dilute (hypotonic) surroundings; the wall normally limits how far the membrane can expand, so "the cell will not lyse." Removing the wall removes that limit. (Choice A) is correct: in the hypotonic freshwater medium, water moves down its concentration gradient into the solute-rich cytoplasm, the cell swells, and with no wall to cap expansion the plasma membrane stretches past its capability and ruptures (osmotic lysis). (Choice B) is incorrect: shrinking and crenation occur when a cell loses water in a hypertonic environment, but here the medium is hypotonic relative to the cytoplasm, so the net water movement is inward, not outward. (Choice C) is incorrect: the plasma membrane is selectively permeable but cannot itself prevent net water entry or withstand the resulting pressure; it is the wall, not the membrane, that normally keeps the cell from lysing. (Choice D) is incorrect: without the rigid wall there is no structure to contain the rising internal pressure, so the cell does not reach a stable swollen state but instead bursts, and losing shape without lysis describes a walled cell, not a wall-less one. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between cytoplasmic solute concentration, osmotic water movement, and the wall''s role in preventing lysis to predict the fate of a wall-less cell in a hypotonic medium.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'a9e850d3-9848-40f9-abac-3916a8de6f42';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'a9e850d3-9848-40f9-abac-3916a8de6f42';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('a9e850d3-9848-40f9-abac-3916a8de6f42', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'swapped hypotonic and hypertonic direction of water flow'),
  ('a9e850d3-9848-40f9-abac-3916a8de6f42', 'C', 'misconception', 'attributes lysis prevention to the plasma membrane rather than the cell wall'),
  ('a9e850d3-9848-40f9-abac-3916a8de6f42', 'D', 'misconception', 'assumes internal turgor pressure alone can stabilize a wall-less cell');

-- [Endomembrane System & Organelle Identification] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A plasma cell in the immune system dedicates most of its output to synthesizing and exporting large quantities of antibody protein into the bloodstream. Compared with a resting lymphocyte that secretes little protein, which structural feature would most likely be dramatically expanded in the plasma cell?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The rough endoplasmic reticulum, whose cytoplasmic surface is studded with ribosomes that feed newly made chains into its lumen"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The smooth endoplasmic reticulum, which lacks surface ribosomes and specializes in lipid and steroid-hormone synthesis"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The peroxisome network, which uses oxidation reactions to break down fatty acids and detoxify the resulting hydrogen peroxide"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The inner mitochondrial membrane, which consumes oxygen during the oxidative reactions that generate ATP"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).'' The endomembrane system modifies, packages, and transports proteins and lipids, and proteins destined for secretion enter this system at the rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER), whose ribosome-studded cytoplasmic surface transfers newly made proteins into the RER lumen for folding and modification (lines 5684-5685). The textbook notes that because the RER modifies proteins that are secreted from the cell, "the RER is abundant in cells that secrete proteins" (lines 5699-5709). A cell whose job is to mass-produce and export a single protein must scale up the very compartment where that protein is made and enters the secretory route. (Choice A) is correct: the ribosomes studding the RER surface synthesize secreted antibodies, which are folded within the RER lumen before traveling to the Golgi and out of the cell, so a high-output secretory cell expands its RER above what a non-secreting cell needs (lines 5684-5685, 5699-5709). (Choice B) is incorrect: the smooth ER lacks bound ribosomes and specializes in lipid, carbohydrate, and steroid-hormone synthesis plus detoxification (lines 5711-5714), so it would be expanded in a steroid-producing or detoxifying cell, not in one exporting a protein. (Choice C) is incorrect: peroxisomes carry out oxidation reactions that break down fatty acids and amino acids and detoxify the resulting hydrogen peroxide (lines 5507-5512); they are not part of the endomembrane secretory route and are not scaled up by protein-export demand. (Choice D) is incorrect: the inner mitochondrial membrane supports ATP-generating oxidative reactions and would expand in an energy-demanding cell, but mitochondria are explicitly excluded from the endomembrane system (line 5653) and do not synthesize or package secreted proteins. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the function of each organelle to a novel secretory scenario and infer which compartment a protein-exporting cell would expand, rather than recalling a labeled fact.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '34790fb1-18bc-499a-b2db-3a3031984b80';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '34790fb1-18bc-499a-b2db-3a3031984b80';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('34790fb1-18bc-499a-b2db-3a3031984b80', 'B', 'misconception', 'confusing rough vs smooth ER function'),
  ('34790fb1-18bc-499a-b2db-3a3031984b80', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'real organelle function unrelated to protein secretion'),
  ('34790fb1-18bc-499a-b2db-3a3031984b80', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'high-workload cell implies mitochondria');

-- [Prokaryotic Genetics: Conjugation] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher grows two strains of E. coli together in one flask: a donor strain carrying a gene for ampicillin resistance and a recipient strain sensitive to ampicillin. After several hours, some recipient cells become ampicillin-resistant. To identify the transfer mechanism, she repeats the experiment but separates the two strains with a fine filter that blocks passage of whole cells while allowing free DNA and virus-sized particles to pass. With the filter in place, no recipient cells acquire resistance. Which mechanism of horizontal gene transfer is BEST supported by these results?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Conjugation, in which DNA passes from the donor to the recipient through a pilus that requires direct contact between the two cells."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Transformation, in which the recipient takes up resistance-gene DNA released into the surrounding medium by the donor cells."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Transduction, in which a bacteriophage packages the resistance gene in the donor and injects it into the recipient cell."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Binary fission, in which the resistance gene passes to new cells as the donor strain replicates and divides into daughter cells."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).'' Prokaryotes share genes horizontally by three mechanisms: transformation (uptake of naked DNA from the environment), transduction (transfer by a bacteriophage), and conjugation, in which DNA moves from one cell to another through a pilus that "requires direct contact" between the two bacteria. The filter is the decisive clue: it lets free DNA and virus-sized particles through but blocks whole cells, so the loss of transfer when contact is prevented points to a mechanism that depends on cell-to-cell contact. (Choice A) is correct: conjugation transfers DNA through a pilus/mating bridge and requires the donor and recipient to be in direct physical contact, so blocking cell passage while still permitting DNA and phage to cross abolishes transfer, exactly as observed. (Choice B) is incorrect: in transformation the recipient takes up DNA shed into the medium, and free DNA passes through the filter, so transfer would still occur despite the barrier. (Choice C) is incorrect: in transduction a virus-sized bacteriophage carries the gene, and such particles pass through the filter, so a phage-mediated route would not be blocked. (Choice D) is incorrect: binary fission is asexual reproduction that copies a cell''s own DNA to its clones and does not move genes between separate strains, so it cannot explain a sensitive recipient becoming resistant. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the defining requirement of each transfer mechanism to the filter result and infer that only a contact-dependent process fits the data.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '8331459e-d1f9-4404-95f4-07f9d8ee9735';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '8331459e-d1f9-4404-95f4-07f9d8ee9735';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('8331459e-d1f9-4404-95f4-07f9d8ee9735', 'B', 'misconception', 'confusing conjugation with transformation (naked-DNA uptake)'),
  ('8331459e-d1f9-4404-95f4-07f9d8ee9735', 'C', 'misconception', 'confusing conjugation with transduction (phage-mediated transfer)'),
  ('8331459e-d1f9-4404-95f4-07f9d8ee9735', 'D', 'misconception', 'attributing horizontal gene transfer to vertical inheritance via binary fission');

-- [Golgi Apparatus] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher uses a drug that specifically blocks vesicles from budding off the trans face of the Golgi apparatus in a salivary gland cell, while leaving cis-face fusion and movement through the Golgi cisternae intact. Which outcome for newly synthesized secretory proteins is most consistent with the normal organization of the Golgi?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Secretory proteins accumulate within the Golgi and fail to reach the plasma membrane for release"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Secretory proteins are prevented from entering the Golgi and instead pile up inside the endoplasmic reticulum"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Secretory proteins are released from the cell but arrive at the surface without their usual carbohydrate modifications"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Secretory proteins bud from the cis face instead and travel backward toward the endoplasmic reticulum"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).'' The Golgi apparatus is a stack of flattened sacs with a defined direction of traffic: transport vesicles from the ER fuse with the cis face (the side nearer the ER), cargo is progressively modified as it passes through the cisternae, and finished proteins are packaged into secretory vesicles that bud from the trans face toward the plasma membrane or other destinations (lines 5764-5773). Because the trans face is the sole exit point for finished cargo, blocking budding there traps proteins that have already entered and been processed. (Choice A) is correct: with the trans-face exit blocked but entry and internal transit intact, cargo still enters at the cis face and moves through the Golgi but has nowhere to go, so it accumulates inside the Golgi and never reaches the plasma membrane for secretion. (Choice B) is incorrect: this reverses the geography of the block; ER-derived vesicles fuse at the cis face, which the drug leaves intact, so proteins still enter the Golgi normally and do not back up into the ER. (Choice C) is incorrect: modification (such as adding short sugar chains) occurs as cargo travels through the cisternae, a step the drug does not touch; the block is only at the exit, so any protein that could get out would already be modified, and in fact secretion is halted entirely. (Choice D) is incorrect: the cis face is an entry point where ER vesicles fuse, not an alternative exit, and Golgi traffic moves cis-to-trans, not backward; the textbook describes finished vesicles budding only from the trans face. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the cis-to-trans directional organization of the Golgi to a novel experimental block and predict where cargo will be stranded.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'ed12b9d5-54a0-4590-a524-cf9645b65260';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'ed12b9d5-54a0-4590-a524-cf9645b65260';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('ed12b9d5-54a0-4590-a524-cf9645b65260', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'swapping the cis (entry) and trans (exit) faces of the Golgi'),
  ('ed12b9d5-54a0-4590-a524-cf9645b65260', 'C', 'misconception', 'conflating a block on Golgi exit with a defect in Golgi modification'),
  ('ed12b9d5-54a0-4590-a524-cf9645b65260', 'D', 'misconception', 'treating the cis face as an alternate exit and imagining backward Golgi traffic');

-- [Prokaryotic Cell Wall & Gram Staining] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher enzymatically strips the cell wall from a rod-shaped bacterium, producing wall-less cells that retain an intact plasma membrane. Equal samples of these wall-less cells are transferred into three media that differ only in solute concentration: a hypotonic medium, an isotonic medium, and a hypertonic medium. In which medium (or media) are the wall-less cells most likely to burst, and why?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Only the hypotonic medium, because water enters the cell and the plasma membrane alone cannot resist the resulting expansion."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Both the hypotonic and isotonic media, because removing the wall eliminates the shape that normally lets the cell tolerate any water uptake."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Only the hypertonic medium, because water leaving the cell raises the internal solute concentration until the membrane ruptures."}, {"label": "D", "text": "None of the media, because the plasma membrane is freely permeable to water and keeps the internal and external concentrations equal."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).'' The bacterial cell wall is a protective layer located outside the plasma membrane that "prevents osmotic lysis (bursting due to increasing volume)"; because the cytoplasm holds a high concentration of dissolved solutes, water tends to enter the cell, and the wall limits how far the plasma membrane can expand. The text notes that walled organisms "prevent cell lysis in a hypotonic solution" because "the plasma membrane can only expand to the cell wall''s limit, so the cell will not lyse." Removing the wall therefore only matters when water is actually driven inward, which occurs in a hypotonic medium. (Choice A) is correct: in a hypotonic medium water flows into the solute-rich cytoplasm, and without the wall to cap membrane expansion the plasma membrane stretches until it ruptures, causing lysis. (Choice B) is incorrect: in an isotonic medium there is no net water movement, so the cell neither swells nor bursts even without a wall; the wall''s shape-giving role is real but is not what protects against bursting when there is no net influx of water. (Choice C) is incorrect: in a hypertonic medium water leaves the cell, so it shrinks (crenates) rather than swelling; concentrating the internal solutes impairs function but does not rupture the membrane by expansion. (Choice D) is incorrect: the plasma membrane is permeable to water but not to most solutes, so osmosis moves water down its gradient rather than equalizing concentrations; a net influx still occurs in hypotonic conditions. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the direction of osmotic water movement together with the wall''s mechanical role to predict when a wall-less cell lyses, rather than recalling that the wall prevents lysis.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '6c1ffb23-02c9-4929-9a20-69d8100cc362';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '6c1ffb23-02c9-4929-9a20-69d8100cc362';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('6c1ffb23-02c9-4929-9a20-69d8100cc362', 'B', 'misconception', 'assumes wall removal makes the cell vulnerable in all non-hypertonic conditions'),
  ('6c1ffb23-02c9-4929-9a20-69d8100cc362', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'swaps swelling/lysis for shrinking, predicts bursting from water loss'),
  ('6c1ffb23-02c9-4929-9a20-69d8100cc362', 'D', 'misconception', 'believes membrane permeability equalizes solute concentrations and prevents osmosis');

-- [Prokaryotic Genetics: Conjugation] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher grows a strain of Escherichia coli carrying an antibiotic-resistance gene together with a susceptible strain lacking it. When the two strains share the same culture medium and are free to touch, some susceptible cells acquire resistance. The researcher then repeats the experiment with the two strains separated by a fine membrane that blocks whole cells from crossing but freely passes naked DNA fragments and virus-sized particles. No susceptible cell acquires resistance. Which conclusion is best supported by these results?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The resistance gene was transferred by conjugation, which depends on direct physical contact between the two cells."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The resistance gene was transferred by transformation, in which cells take up DNA released into the surrounding medium."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The resistance gene was transferred by transduction, in which a bacteriophage carries the gene from one cell to another."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The resistance gene arose by spontaneous mutation in the susceptible strain rather than by any transfer between cells."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).'' Prokaryotes share genes by three mechanisms: transformation (uptake of naked DNA from the medium), transduction (transfer of DNA by a bacteriophage), and conjugation, in which "DNA is transferred from one prokaryote to another by means of a pilus, which brings the organisms into contact with one another, and provides a channel for transfer of DNA." The membrane in this experiment blocks whole cells but still passes free DNA and virus-sized particles, so it acts as a control that isolates the requirement for direct cell-to-cell contact. (Choice A) is correct: because transfer succeeded only when the cells could touch and failed when a membrane allowed free DNA and phage through but prevented contact, the mechanism must be the one that requires a pilus-mediated bridge between cells, which is conjugation. (Choice B) is incorrect: transformation relies on DNA released into the medium, so it would still occur through a membrane that freely passes naked DNA; the loss of transfer under those conditions rules it out. (Choice C) is incorrect: transduction is mediated by bacteriophages, which are virus-sized and would pass through a membrane permeable to virus-sized particles, so transfer would persist rather than stop. (Choice D) is incorrect: a spontaneous mutation would appear at some low rate regardless of contact between the strains, so it cannot explain why resistance appeared only when the cells were allowed to touch. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinguishing requirements of the three prokaryotic gene-transfer mechanisms to interpret the membrane-separation control and identify which mechanism is responsible.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '2a3c8980-dbf3-4130-9da9-b9c54c62e429';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '2a3c8980-dbf3-4130-9da9-b9c54c62e429';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('2a3c8980-dbf3-4130-9da9-b9c54c62e429', 'B', 'misconception', 'confuses conjugation with transformation (contact-dependent vs. free-DNA uptake)'),
  ('2a3c8980-dbf3-4130-9da9-b9c54c62e429', 'C', 'misconception', 'confuses conjugation with transduction (contact-dependent vs. phage-mediated)'),
  ('2a3c8980-dbf3-4130-9da9-b9c54c62e429', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'attributes acquired resistance to de novo mutation instead of horizontal transfer');

-- [Tissues: Epithelial Tissue] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher studies a sheet of epithelial cells lining a fluid-filled cavity. She adds a small fluorescent tracer to the fluid on the apical (cavity-facing) side and finds that none of it seeps through the narrow spaces between adjacent cells to reach the basal side, even though the cells are not physically fused. When she treats the sheet with an agent that disrupts one specific type of intercellular junction, the tracer begins leaking freely through the gaps between cells, yet the cells remain firmly anchored to one another and continue to stretch as a cohesive sheet. Which junction did the agent most likely disrupt?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Tight junctions"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Desmosomes"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Gap junctions"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Plasmodesmata"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).'' Epithelial cells are joined by intercellular junctions that serve distinct jobs: some seal the space between cells, some mechanically fasten cells together, and some open channels for communication. The observation to explain is a barrier failure specifically in the paracellular route (the narrow gaps between cells) that occurs without loss of mechanical cohesion. (Choice A) is correct: a tight junction is "a watertight seal between two adjacent animal cells" whose "tight adherence prevents materials from leaking between the cells," which is exactly why "the tight junctions of the epithelial cells lining your urinary bladder prevent urine from leaking out into the extracellular space" [lines 6115-6127]; disrupting only this seal would let the tracer leak through the paracellular gaps while leaving the mechanical anchoring intact. (Choice B) is incorrect: desmosomes "act like spot welds between adjacent epithelial cells" using cadherins linked to intermediate filaments and "maintain the cells in a sheet-like formation in organs and tissues that stretch" [lines 6130-6141]; they provide mechanical strength, not a paracellular seal, so losing them would loosen the sheet rather than open a leak while cohesion is preserved. (Choice C) is incorrect: gap junctions are connexin-lined "channels between adjacent cells that allow for transporting ions, nutrients, and other substances that enable cells to communicate" [lines 6150-6151]; they move small molecules through the cytoplasm from cell to cell, not fluid through the spaces between cells, so their loss would impair intercellular communication rather than cause a paracellular leak. (Choice D) is incorrect: plasmodesmata are "junctions between plant cells" that pass through the cell walls [lines 6096, 6099-6104]; animal epithelium has no cell walls and no plasmodesmata, so they cannot be the disrupted structure. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the specific function of each junction type to a novel experimental observation (a selective paracellular leak with preserved mechanical cohesion) to deduce which junction was disrupted.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
