-- [Prokaryotic Genetics: Conjugation] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In an experiment, a donor strain of \(E.\ coli\) carrying only the F (fertility) plasmid is mixed with an F− recipient strain that lacks the plasmid but carries a chromosomal streptomycin-resistance gene. After ample time for conjugation, the mixed culture is examined. Assuming typical F-plasmid conjugation (no Hfr formation), which outcome is expected for the recipient population?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The recipient cells become F+ donors able to transfer the F plasmid to remaining F− cells."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The donor cells lose the F plasmid and are converted into F− recipient cells."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The donor''s chromosomal genes are co-transferred with the F plasmid into the recipients."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The donor and recipient chromosomes recombine, giving the recipients an entirely new genotype."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).'' In F-plasmid conjugation, the F pilus draws an F+ donor and an F− recipient together, a cytoplasmic bridge forms, and rolling-circle replication feeds a single strand of the F plasmid into the recipient, which then makes it double-stranded. Because rolling-circle replication leaves an intact copy of the plasmid behind, the donor stays F+ while the recipient is newly converted to F+. (Choice A) is correct: the textbook states that once the transferred strand is made double-stranded, "The F− cell now becomes an F+ cell capable of making its own conjugation pilus," and that "in a mixed bacterial population... all cells will become F+ cells" — so the former recipients are themselves now donors that can pass the plasmid onward. (Choice B) is incorrect: this reflects the common misconception that transfer means giving the plasmid away; in fact rolling-circle replication copies the plasmid, so the donor retains its F plasmid and remains F+. (Choice C) is incorrect: "typical conjugation in E. coli results in the transfer of the F-plasmid DNA only." Chromosomal genes (like the resistance gene) are co-transferred only when the F plasmid integrates into the chromosome to form an Hfr cell, which the stem explicitly excludes. (Choice D) is incorrect: bacterial conjugation is not eukaryotic sexual reproduction; ordinary F+ transfer moves a plasmid, not paired chromosomes, so no chromosomal recombination or new hybrid genotype is generated in the recipient. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the mechanism of rolling-circle F-plasmid transfer to predict the genetic state of the recipient population, rather than recall the name of an appendage.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'f9afe4f4-d13f-494b-b88f-1e648857cf9e';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'f9afe4f4-d13f-494b-b88f-1e648857cf9e';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('f9afe4f4-d13f-494b-b88f-1e648857cf9e', 'B', 'misconception', 'transfer means the donor gives away and loses the plasmid'),
  ('f9afe4f4-d13f-494b-b88f-1e648857cf9e', 'C', 'misconception', 'conflating ordinary F+ conjugation with Hfr chromosomal transfer'),
  ('f9afe4f4-d13f-494b-b88f-1e648857cf9e', 'D', 'misconception', 'treating bacterial conjugation like eukaryotic sexual recombination');

-- [Cytoskeleton: Intermediate Filaments] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Skin cells from a patient with a severe blistering disorder carry a mutation in the gene for keratin. When these epithelial cells are grown in culture and gently poked, stretched, or handled, they tear apart far more easily than normal skin cells, even though they still migrate across the dish, divide, and shuttle vesicles through the cytoplasm at normal rates. Which cytoskeletal component''s normal role is MOST likely lost in these cells?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The component that resists mechanical tension to keep the cell from pulling apart"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The component that forms tracks for the long-range transport of vesicles"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The component that assembles a ring to divide one cell into two"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The component that rapidly disassembles and reforms to let the cell crawl"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).'' The cytoskeleton has three fiber types with distinct jobs: microfilaments (actin) drive movement and the contractile ring of cytokinesis, microtubules (tubulin) form tracks for vesicle transport and pull chromosomes apart, and intermediate filaments are the diverse, purely structural group that OpenStax says "bear tension, thus maintaining the cell''s shape, and anchor the nucleus and other organelles in place" (Ch.4.5). Keratin is the textbook''s named example of an intermediate-filament protein, one that "strengthens your hair, nails, and the skin''s epidermis." (Choice A) is correct: a keratin defect removes an intermediate filament, and the phenotype is a purely mechanical one — cells that tear when strained but still move, divide, and traffic vesicles — which matches the tension-bearing structural role that intermediate filaments alone provide. (Choice B) is incorrect: long-range vesicle transport runs on microtubules, which "provide a track along which vesicles move through the cell"; the stem states vesicle shuttling is still normal, so this microtubule function is intact, and keratin is not a tubulin polymer. (Choice C) is incorrect: cytokinesis is carried out by an actin contractile ring, an event the text lists among the motions actin powers "such as cell division"; the cells still divide normally, so this microfilament role is preserved. (Choice D) is incorrect: crawling depends on microfilaments that "depolymerize (disassemble) and reform quickly, thus enabling a cell to change its shape and move," and the cells still migrate across the dish; moreover the text states intermediate filaments "have no role in cell movement," so this dynamic motility function was never theirs to lose. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the functional profile of each cytoskeletal fiber to a phenotype — recognizing that a defect causing mechanical fragility while leaving movement, division, and transport intact points to the purely structural, tension-bearing intermediate filaments, without the stem naming that class.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '5e881100-8821-45b7-9cd4-22a4cd759d9a';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '5e881100-8821-45b7-9cd4-22a4cd759d9a';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('5e881100-8821-45b7-9cd4-22a4cd759d9a', 'B', 'misconception', 'confusing_microtubule_transport_with_structural_support'),
  ('5e881100-8821-45b7-9cd4-22a4cd759d9a', 'C', 'misconception', 'attributing_cytokinesis_to_wrong_filament'),
  ('5e881100-8821-45b7-9cd4-22a4cd759d9a', 'D', 'misconception', 'assigning_motility_to_intermediate_filaments');

-- [Prokaryotic Genetics: Transduction] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher mixes an antibiotic-resistant Bacillus donor strain with a susceptible recipient strain and measures how often resistant recipients arise under three conditions. (1) Adding DNase to the medium does not reduce the appearance of resistant recipients. (2) When the two strains are kept in separate compartments divided by a filter whose pores block bacterial cells but allow much smaller particles to pass, resistant recipients still arise. (3) Adding antiserum that binds and inactivates the donor strain''s bacteriophages abolishes the appearance of resistant recipients. Which mechanism of gene transfer is most consistent with all three observations?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Transduction — the resistance gene is delivered from donor to recipient inside a viral particle"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Transformation — the recipient takes up free resistance-gene DNA from the surrounding medium"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Conjugation — the resistance gene passes through a pilus during direct donor-recipient contact"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Transposition — the resistance gene relocates within the recipient''s own genome via a mobile element"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).'' Prokaryotes exchange genes by three classic horizontal-transfer routes that differ in their physical requirements: transformation depends on naked DNA being present in the medium, conjugation requires direct cell-to-cell contact through a pilus, and transduction moves DNA between cells packaged inside a bacteriophage. Because each route has a distinguishing physical property, the three experimental treatments together fingerprint the mechanism rather than relying on any single result. (Choice A) is correct: DNase resistance shows the transferred DNA is shielded from nuclease (consistent with being inside a phage capsid, not naked); transfer across a filter that blocks cells but passes smaller particles shows no cell-to-cell contact is needed yet a small particle must cross; and loss of transfer after antiserum inactivates the phages pins the vector specifically to bacteriophages — this is transduction, in which "a bacteriophage injects DNA into the cell that contains a small fragment of DNA from a different prokaryote" (OpenStax ~line 26336). (Choice B) is incorrect: transformation requires the recipient to "take up naked DNA" shed into the environment (~line 24112), but naked DNA would be degraded by DNase, so a DNase-resistant transfer rules transformation out. (Choice C) is incorrect: conjugation transfers genes through "a hollow tube, or pilus" that requires the two cells to be drawn into direct contact (~line 24114, 26321), so transfer that still occurs when a filter physically separates the cells excludes conjugation. (Choice D) is incorrect: transposition relocates a mobile element within or between DNA molecules inside a single cell and does not itself move a gene from one separate cell to another, so it cannot explain donor-to-recipient transfer that depends on an external phage vector. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinguishing physical properties of each transfer route to a set of experimental results and infer which mechanism is operating.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'ec2521ef-c5fd-4314-abfd-be57c2a9ba53';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'ec2521ef-c5fd-4314-abfd-be57c2a9ba53';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('ec2521ef-c5fd-4314-abfd-be57c2a9ba53', 'B', 'misconception', 'confuses DNase-resistant phage-packaged DNA with naked environmental DNA uptake'),
  ('ec2521ef-c5fd-4314-abfd-be57c2a9ba53', 'C', 'misconception', 'ignores that transfer occurred despite a barrier blocking cell-to-cell contact'),
  ('ec2521ef-c5fd-4314-abfd-be57c2a9ba53', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'conflates intracellular mobile-element movement with inter-cell horizontal gene transfer');

-- [Prokaryotic Growth & Metabolism] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A microbiologist isolates a soil bacterium that grows steadily in a sealed vessel from which all oxygen has been excluded. Under these conditions the culture consumes sulfate from the medium and releases hydrogen sulfide, and its growth is abolished when the researcher adds a compound that blocks electron flow through the membrane-bound electron transport chain. Which description best characterizes how this bacterium meets its energy needs?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "It carries out anaerobic respiration, passing electrons down an electron transport chain to sulfate as the terminal electron acceptor."}, {"label": "B", "text": "It relies on fermentation, regenerating NAD+ by transferring electrons to an organic molecule rather than to an electron transport chain."}, {"label": "C", "text": "It performs aerobic respiration in which sulfate substitutes for oxygen at the final cytochrome of the electron transport chain."}, {"label": "D", "text": "It produces all of its ATP by substrate-level phosphorylation during glycolysis, without using an electron transport chain."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).'' Prokaryotes are metabolically diverse: their energy-producing pathways may be aerobic, using oxygen as the terminal electron acceptor, or anaerobic, using an inorganic or organic molecule as the terminal electron acceptor. In anaerobic cellular respiration the electron transport chain still operates, but it passes electrons to an inorganic final acceptor; sulfate-reducing bacteria, for example, reduce sulfate to hydrogen sulfide to regenerate NAD+ from NADH. (Choice A) is correct: the culture grows without oxygen, generates hydrogen sulfide from sulfate, and stops growing when the electron transport chain is blocked, which together indicate a functioning chain that terminates on sulfate as an inorganic electron acceptor — the defining feature of anaerobic respiration. (Choice B) is incorrect: fermentation regenerates NAD+ by handing electrons to an organic molecule and uses no electron transport chain, so a fermenting cell would be unaffected by an inhibitor of chain electron flow, contradicting the observation. (Choice C) is incorrect: aerobic respiration is defined by oxygen serving as the terminal electron acceptor, and this vessel contains no oxygen; sulfate is not simply plugged into an oxygen-requiring aerobic pathway but is the acceptor of a distinct anaerobic one. (Choice D) is incorrect: relying solely on substrate-level phosphorylation in glycolysis would not require a membrane electron transport chain, so blocking that chain would not abolish growth as it does here. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definitions of aerobic respiration, anaerobic respiration, and fermentation to the experimental observations and infer which pathway is consistent with growth without oxygen, sulfide production, and dependence on an intact electron transport chain.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '710f7b2b-71fa-47f8-875f-04d88ae106cb';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '710f7b2b-71fa-47f8-875f-04d88ae106cb';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('710f7b2b-71fa-47f8-875f-04d88ae106cb', 'B', 'misconception', 'conflates fermentation with anaerobic respiration'),
  ('710f7b2b-71fa-47f8-875f-04d88ae106cb', 'C', 'misconception', 'treats inorganic acceptor as a drop-in substitute within aerobic respiration'),
  ('710f7b2b-71fa-47f8-875f-04d88ae106cb', 'D', 'misconception', 'assumes all oxygen-independent ATP comes from glycolytic substrate-level phosphorylation');

-- [Tissues: Connective Tissue] ans=D medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Six weeks after a deep laceration to a patient''s forearm, a biopsy of the healed dermis shows a firm scar: the region is now packed with densely woven collagen fibers embedded in ground substance, giving the skin high tensile strength but noticeably less flexibility than the surrounding uninjured tissue. Which resident connective-tissue cell was chiefly responsible for producing the new fibers and ground substance seen in this scar?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The mast cell, which stores and releases histamine and heparin during tissue irritation or damage"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The macrophage, which migrates in from the bloodstream and engulfs pathogens and cellular debris"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The chondrocyte, which resides in lacunae and produces the matrix and fibers of cartilage"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The fibroblast, which is the chief connective-tissue-proper cell and makes its fibers and ground substance"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'D',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).'' Connective tissue proper is built from a matrix of protein fibers (collagen, elastic, reticular) suspended in a ground substance, and the textbook identifies the fibroblast as "the principal cell of connective tissues," the cell that "makes the fibers found in nearly all of the connective tissues." A dermal scar is dense fibrous connective tissue — the same fiber-and-ground-substance matrix that fibroblasts normally deposit and repair — so the cell responsible for laying down the new collagen must be reasoned back to from among several genuinely resident connective-tissue cells. (Choice A) is incorrect: the mast cell''s granules "contain the chemical signals histamine and heparin," inflammatory mediators it releases when tissue is irritated or damaged; it participates in the acute response but does not synthesize the structural fibers or ground substance of the scar. (Choice B) is incorrect: the macrophage is a phagocyte that enters from the blood and functions in "engulfing infectious agents and cellular debris," clearing the wound rather than depositing the collagen matrix that gives the scar its strength. (Choice C) is incorrect: the chondrocyte does make matrix and fibers, but it does so within cartilage, sitting in lacunae — it is not a resident of dermal connective tissue proper and does not build a skin scar. (Choice D) is correct: the fibroblast is the principal connective-tissue-proper cell, and the collagen fibers plus polysaccharide-rich ground substance forming the strong, less-flexible scar are exactly the extracellular matrix components fibroblasts synthesize and secrete. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply your knowledge of each connective-tissue cell''s distinct function to an observed healing outcome and infer which cell produced the collagen-rich matrix, rather than recognizing a cell from a definition handed to you.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '2b550209-9748-4120-b49a-bffa51b34c2a';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '2b550209-9748-4120-b49a-bffa51b34c2a';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('2b550209-9748-4120-b49a-bffa51b34c2a', 'A', 'misconception', 'wrong-cell-right-tissue'),
  ('2b550209-9748-4120-b49a-bffa51b34c2a', 'B', 'misconception', 'wrong-cell-right-tissue'),
  ('2b550209-9748-4120-b49a-bffa51b34c2a', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'correct-function-wrong-tissue');

-- [Viral Genomes and Replication Strategy] ans=B hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher works with two enveloped, non-segmented ssRNA animal viruses. Virus X carries a positive-sense (\(+\)) ssRNA genome; Virus Y carries a negative-sense (\(-\)) ssRNA genome. From each virus the researcher strips away all viral proteins and transfects the purified, naked genomic RNA into fresh, uninfected host cells. Cells receiving Virus X''s naked RNA go on to produce infectious progeny virions, but cells receiving Virus Y''s naked RNA produce none. Which statement BEST explains this difference in outcome?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Virus Y''s genome must be reverse transcribed into DNA before any viral gene can be expressed, and the naked RNA cannot accomplish that conversion on its own."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Virus X''s genome can be translated directly by host ribosomes, whereas Virus Y''s genome must first be transcribed by a polymerase the stripped RNA no longer carries."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Virus X''s genome remains shielded by its capsid after transfection, whereas Virus Y''s genome is left exposed and is degraded before it can act."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Virus X''s genome integrates into the host chromosome to direct progeny production, whereas Virus Y''s genome cannot integrate and is therefore lost."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).'' The Baltimore classification groups RNA viruses by how each makes mRNA: a positive-sense ssRNA genome "can serve directly as mRNA," but a negative-sense ssRNA genome has a sequence "complementary to the mRNA" and must first be copied into mRNA using a virus-encoded RNA-dependent RNA polymerase (lines 24801-24810). The task here is to reason from an experiment—naked genome transfection—to why one strategy launches infection and the other does not. (Choice A) is incorrect: reverse transcription into DNA is the strategy of retroviruses (Baltimore Group VI), not of ordinary negative-sense ssRNA viruses such as Virus Y; a negative-sense RNA virus replicates through RNA intermediates and never makes a DNA copy, so this misidentifies the missing step. (Choice B) is correct: because Virus X''s genome is positive-sense, host ribosomes can immediately translate it into the viral proteins (including its polymerase) needed to begin replication, so the naked RNA alone is infectious; Virus Y''s genome is the complement of mRNA and cannot be read by ribosomes, so it must be transcribed first by the RNA-dependent RNA polymerase that is normally packaged in the virion—an enzyme that was discarded when the proteins were stripped, leaving the naked RNA inert. (Choice C) is incorrect: both genomes were stripped of all proteins, so neither retains a capsid, and a bare genome''s fate does not hinge on a capsid that is no longer present; the difference lies in polarity, not in protection from degradation. (Choice D) is incorrect: neither an ordinary positive- nor negative-sense ssRNA virus integrates into the host chromosome—integration is characteristic of retroviruses—so integration cannot be what distinguishes these two viruses. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that positive-sense RNA acts directly as mRNA while negative-sense RNA requires a packaged polymerase to interpret a novel transfection experiment.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '12b57c52-ba0b-4f12-b0bc-2cf8f19d8511';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '12b57c52-ba0b-4f12-b0bc-2cf8f19d8511';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('12b57c52-ba0b-4f12-b0bc-2cf8f19d8511', 'A', 'misconception', 'conflates negative-sense RNA replication with retroviral reverse transcription'),
  ('12b57c52-ba0b-4f12-b0bc-2cf8f19d8511', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'invokes capsid protection when both genomes were stripped of all proteins'),
  ('12b57c52-ba0b-4f12-b0bc-2cf8f19d8511', 'D', 'misconception', 'attributes host-genome integration to a non-retroviral RNA virus');

-- [Mitochondria] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In one cultured cell line, a mutation causes the inner mitochondrial membrane to lose its characteristic folds, so the inner membrane becomes a smooth, unfolded sphere while the outer membrane, the intermembrane space, and the total volume of the central interior are all unchanged. Compared with a normal mitochondrion of the same overall size, which functional consequence would this change most directly produce?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "A greater capacity to hold citric acid cycle enzymes in the central interior"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A reduced capacity for the ATP-synthesizing machinery embedded in the inner membrane"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A larger intermembrane space that stores more hydrogen ions at rest"}, {"label": "D", "text": "An increased ability of the outer membrane to import pyruvate from the cytosol"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).'' The mitochondrion is a double-membrane organelle whose inner membrane is thrown into folds called cristae; the text notes that these folds "increase its surface area" and that "ATP synthesis takes place on the inner membrane." Because the machinery that makes ATP sits within the inner membrane itself, the amount of that membrane available sets the ceiling on how much ATP-synthesizing machinery the organelle can hold. (Choice A) is incorrect: the citric acid cycle enzymes are dissolved in the matrix (the central interior), whose volume is stated to be unchanged, so capacity for those enzymes is not affected by flattening the membrane. (Choice B) is correct: flattening the cristae removes the folds that increase inner-membrane surface area, and since the ATP-synthesizing complexes are embedded in that membrane, less surface area means fewer such complexes can be housed, lowering ATP-production capacity. (Choice C) is incorrect: the intermembrane space lies outside the inner membrane and is bounded by the unchanged outer membrane; smoothing the inner membrane does not enlarge it, and hydrogen ions are actively pumped there rather than stored "at rest." (Choice D) is incorrect: it is the outer membrane, not the folded inner membrane, that faces the cytosol, and the mutation is specified to leave the outer membrane unchanged, so import across it is not enhanced. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between inner-membrane folding, membrane surface area, and the inner-membrane location of ATP synthesis to predict the consequence of losing the cristae.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '7938be20-fc93-41d6-b205-59c51d75cff2';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '7938be20-fc93-41d6-b205-59c51d75cff2';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('7938be20-fc93-41d6-b205-59c51d75cff2', 'A', 'misconception', 'confusing_membrane_folding_with_matrix_volume'),
  ('7938be20-fc93-41d6-b205-59c51d75cff2', 'C', 'misconception', 'misattributing_folding_effect_to_intermembrane_space'),
  ('7938be20-fc93-41d6-b205-59c51d75cff2', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'swapping_inner_and_outer_membrane_roles');

-- [Prokaryotic Cell Wall & Gram Staining] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A rod-shaped bacterium suspended in distilled water keeps its shape and stays intact. When researchers treat the cells with an enzyme that hydrolyzes the peptidoglycan wall, the wall-free cells round up and burst while still in the distilled water. If, instead, the wall is digested while the cells sit in a medium made isotonic to their cytoplasm, the cells round up but survive. Which statement best explains why removing the wall causes lysis in distilled water but not in the isotonic medium?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "In distilled water the high internal solute concentration draws water in, and without the wall to resist the swelling the membrane stretches until it lyses; in the isotonic medium there is no net water entry, so the wall-free cells hold together."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The wall is what draws water into the cell, so removing it in distilled water lets water rush in with nothing to pull against, whereas the isotonic medium takes over the wall''s water-pulling role and keeps inflow balanced."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The peptidoglycan wall is the cell''s main permeability barrier, so once it is digested the internal solutes leak out into the distilled water, and the sudden collapse of the solute gradient ruptures the membrane."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The wall is needed to anchor the plasma membrane in place, so without it the membrane loses its attachment and disintegrates in any aqueous medium, regardless of how the outside solute concentration compares to the inside."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).'' The cytoplasm of prokaryotic cells has a high concentration of dissolved solutes, so the internal osmotic pressure is relatively high; the cell wall is a rigid protective layer outside the cell membrane that gives the cell shape and, critically, prevents osmotic lysis (bursting due to increasing volume) when the cell sits in a dilute environment. Water crosses the membrane down its own concentration gradient, so in a hypotonic medium water enters the solute-rich cytoplasm; the plasma membrane can expand only to the wall''s limit, and once that wall is gone nothing opposes the swelling. (Choice A) is correct: distilled water is strongly hypotonic to the solute-rich cytoplasm, so water enters and swells the cell; with the wall digested, the membrane stretches past the point it can hold and lyses, whereas in an isotonic medium there is no net water entry and the wall-free cell simply rounds up and survives. (Choice B) is incorrect: the wall does not draw water in; the osmotic gradient set by the cell''s own dissolved solutes drives water entry, and the isotonic medium prevents lysis by removing the gradient, not by mimicking any water-pulling action of the wall. (Choice C) is incorrect: the plasma membrane, not the porous peptidoglycan wall, is the cell''s selective permeability barrier, so digesting the wall does not let solutes leak out; lysis results from water flowing in, not from a solute gradient collapsing. (Choice D) is incorrect: this is contradicted by the observation that wall-free cells survive intact in the isotonic medium, showing the membrane does not simply disintegrate whenever the wall is absent; the outcome depends on the tonicity of the surrounding medium. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the direction of osmotic water movement and the mechanical role of the cell wall to a novel wall-digestion experiment to predict the result.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'fe8ecdbc-7725-4ef6-8a05-551bea454510';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'fe8ecdbc-7725-4ef6-8a05-551bea454510';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('fe8ecdbc-7725-4ef6-8a05-551bea454510', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'attributes water movement to the wall rather than to the osmotic gradient'),
  ('fe8ecdbc-7725-4ef6-8a05-551bea454510', 'C', 'misconception', 'confuses the porous cell wall with the plasma membrane as the permeability barrier'),
  ('fe8ecdbc-7725-4ef6-8a05-551bea454510', 'D', 'misconception', 'claims the wall is required for membrane integrity in all conditions, ignoring tonicity');

-- [Viral Life Cycle: Lytic and Lysogenic] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A microbiologist adds a temperate bacteriophage to a growing culture of *E. coli*. For the next 40 generations the culture divides normally, and no free virions can be detected in the medium even though every cell carries the phage''s DNA. The culture is then briefly exposed to ultraviolet light, and within an hour the medium fills with newly assembled virions while many host cells die. Which statement BEST accounts for the entire sequence of observations?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The phage integrated its genome into the host chromosome, was copied along with it during each division, and the UV exposure triggered it to excise and enter the lytic cycle."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The phage began replicating lytically at once but produced virions so slowly that they became detectable only after UV sped up the host''s metabolism."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The UV damage forced the actively replicating phage into a protective lysogenic state, and the detected virions were leftovers from the original infection."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The phage persisted as free genomes in the cytoplasm without integrating, and UV merely lysed cells that already held mature virions."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).'' A temperate bacteriophage can replicate by a lysogenic cycle, in which its genome is incorporated into the host chromosome as a prophage; the textbook notes the prophage is then "passed on to subsequent generations," and that "environmental stressors such as starvation or exposure to toxic chemicals may cause the prophage to excise and enter the lytic cycle," in which new phages are produced and the host cell is lysed. The observations must be read together: no free virions for many generations even though every cell carries phage DNA, then a sudden burst of virions and cell death after UV. (Choice A) is correct: quiet inheritance of the phage DNA is the signature of an integrated prophage copied with the host genome each division, and UV is the stressor that induces excision and the switch to the lytic cycle that produces virions and kills cells. (Choice B) is incorrect: an immediate lytic infection would produce virions and kill cells from the start rather than dividing normally for 40 generations, and UV does not accelerate lytic virion output by boosting metabolism. (Choice C) is incorrect: it reverses the actual relationship, because a stressor such as UV drives a prophage out of lysogeny into the lytic cycle, not into it, and the flood of freshly assembled virions cannot be leftovers from the original inoculation. (Choice D) is incorrect: if the genome persisted unintegrated in the cytoplasm it would not be faithfully carried by every daughter cell across 40 divisions, and the virions appeared only after UV rather than pre-existing in the cells. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the two-phase behavior of a temperate phage to infer that a silent, heritable infection is an integrated prophage and that a stressor induces its switch to the lytic cycle.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '4c133b12-01b8-46b5-abc3-2983fe8a0916';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '4c133b12-01b8-46b5-abc3-2983fe8a0916';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('4c133b12-01b8-46b5-abc3-2983fe8a0916', 'B', 'misconception', 'lytic-only reasoning: assumes any productive phage is replicating lytically from the outset'),
  ('4c133b12-01b8-46b5-abc3-2983fe8a0916', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'believes a stressor pushes a phage into lysogeny rather than inducing it out into the lytic cycle'),
  ('4c133b12-01b8-46b5-abc3-2983fe8a0916', 'D', 'misconception', 'conflates episomal/latent persistence with the integrated prophage that is inherited by daughter cells');

-- [Tissues: Epithelial Tissue] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'The epithelium lining the small intestine holds a steep concentration gradient: the lumen is rich in dietary solutes while the tissue fluid beneath is comparatively dilute. A bacterial toxin is applied to this epithelium that specifically dismantles the protein seals joining the apical edges of neighboring epithelial cells, leaving the cells themselves alive and their apical membranes intact. Immediately after the toxin acts, what is the most direct consequence for movement of luminal solutes across the epithelium?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Luminal solutes can slip through the spaces between adjacent cells and enter the tissue fluid below"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Adjacent cells detach from one another and the epithelial sheet peels away from the underlying tissue"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Electrical and chemical signals can no longer pass directly from one epithelial cell to the next"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Solutes are actively pumped across the cells more rapidly to compensate for the disruption"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).'' Epithelial cells line the lumens of organs and are joined by cell-cell junctions, and the tight junctions near their apical edges form watertight seals that prevent materials from leaking through the spaces between neighboring cells. The textbook states that this "tight adherence prevents materials from leaking between the cells," using the epithelial lining of the urinary bladder, whose tight junctions keep urine from seeping into the extracellular space, as its example. (Choice A) is correct: the seals destroyed by the toxin are the tight junctions that normally block the paracellular route, so once they are gone, luminal solutes are free to diffuse down their gradient through the gaps between cells into the tissue fluid below—the barrier that sealed the sheet has been opened. (Choice B) is incorrect: physically anchoring adjacent cells and welding the sheet to underlying tissue is the job of desmosomes (cadherin and intermediate-filament "spot welds"), not the apical seals; the stem specifies the cells remain alive with intact membranes, so the sheet does not peel away. (Choice C) is incorrect: direct passage of ions and signaling molecules between cells occurs through gap junctions (connexon channels), a distinct junction type; losing the apical seals does not close these communication channels. (Choice D) is incorrect: destroying a passive barrier does not upregulate transcellular active transport, and invoking a compensatory pump misreads the immediate effect, which is simply that the paracellular pathway is no longer sealed. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the barrier function of epithelial cell-cell junctions to predict the consequence of removing the apical seal, rather than recall which junction is named.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '3da53106-c4db-4ace-ae07-0dd3daf4fcce';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '3da53106-c4db-4ace-ae07-0dd3daf4fcce';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('3da53106-c4db-4ace-ae07-0dd3daf4fcce', 'B', 'misconception', 'confuses tight junctions with desmosomes/anchoring junctions'),
  ('3da53106-c4db-4ace-ae07-0dd3daf4fcce', 'C', 'misconception', 'confuses tight junctions with gap junctions/communication'),
  ('3da53106-c4db-4ace-ae07-0dd3daf4fcce', 'D', 'misconception', 'assumes cells compensate via active transport after barrier loss');

-- [Viral Genomes and Replication Strategy] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A microbiologist adds a temperate bacteriophage to a growing culture of E. coli. Instead of the culture clearing, most cells survive and keep dividing, and no burst of new virions is detected for many generations. When the researcher sequences descendants isolated much later, the viral genome is still present in nearly every cell, even though those cells were never re-exposed to phage particles. Which mechanism best accounts for how the viral genome is maintained throughout the expanding bacterial population?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The phage genome is integrated into the host chromosome and is copied along with the host DNA, so each daughter cell inherits it at division."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The phage genome persists as an independent cytoplasmic element that directs steady production of virions, which reinfect the daughter cells."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Each infected cell lyses to release new virions, and those particles then infect the neighboring cells throughout the culture."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Host nucleases degrade the incoming phage genome, so only cells that take up free viral DNA from the medium retain it."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).'' A temperate bacteriophage can replicate by a lysogenic cycle in which the viral genome is inserted into the host-cell genome rather than immediately producing and releasing virions. The textbook explains that "when a temperate bacteriophage infects a bacterial cell, it replicates by means of a lysogenic cycle... and the viral genome is incorporated into the genome of the host cell. When the phage DNA is incorporated into the host-cell genome, it is called a prophage," and that in this cycle the "phage DNA is incorporated into the host genome, where it is passed on to subsequent generations" (lines 25009-25030). (Choice A) is correct: as a prophage, the viral DNA is physically part of the bacterial chromosome, so it is duplicated by ordinary host DNA replication and each daughter cell inherits a copy without any new infection event — this explains why the genome spreads through a dividing population that survives and is never re-exposed to phage. (Choice B) is incorrect: this describes ongoing productive/lytic-style virion synthesis and conflates the prophage with a self-replicating plasmid; a lysogenic phage is silent and does not continuously make new particles, which is why no virion burst is seen. (Choice C) is incorrect: cell lysis to release virions is the lytic cycle, but the stem states that most cells survive and keep dividing, so widespread lysis and reinfection cannot be maintaining the genome. (Choice D) is incorrect: if host enzymes destroyed the phage genome it could not be present in nearly every descendant, and uptake of free DNA from the medium (transformation) is not how a prophage is propagated. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction between the lytic and lysogenic cycles to infer that only chromosomal integration (a prophage copied with the host DNA) can carry the viral genome through a surviving, dividing population.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'f81113a2-5cc1-45d2-812e-0ba013373fdc';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'f81113a2-5cc1-45d2-812e-0ba013373fdc';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('f81113a2-5cc1-45d2-812e-0ba013373fdc', 'B', 'misconception', 'conflates lysogeny with continuous virion production / prophage-as-plasmid'),
  ('f81113a2-5cc1-45d2-812e-0ba013373fdc', 'C', 'misconception', 'applies the lytic cycle where the lysogenic cycle is described'),
  ('f81113a2-5cc1-45d2-812e-0ba013373fdc', 'D', 'misconception', 'confuses genome maintenance with nuclease degradation and transformation');

-- [Viral Genomes and Replication Strategy] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'An experimental antiviral drug is designed to bind and block the enzyme that copies the RNA genome of HIV into DNA. In infected patients, the drug sharply reduces production of new virus particles, yet the treated patients'' own uninfected cells continue their normal metabolism with essentially no toxicity from the drug. Which feature of the retroviral life cycle best explains why blocking this copying step harms the virus but spares the host cell?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The enzyme that copies the viral RNA into DNA is supplied by the virus and is not found in uninfected host cells"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The enzyme that copies the viral RNA into DNA is a host polymerase that the cell uses only while dividing"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The step that copies the viral RNA into DNA occurs only after the genome has integrated into the host chromosome"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The step that copies the viral RNA into DNA is one the host cell routinely uses to convert its own mRNA into DNA"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).'' Retroviruses such as HIV carry an RNA genome that must be reverse transcribed into DNA before it can be integrated into the host chromosome, and the textbook stresses that "reverse transcription never occurs in uninfected host cells—the enzyme reverse transcriptase is only derived from the expression of viral genes within the infected host cells" (lines 24939–24941). Because "HIV produces some of its own enzymes not found in the host," researchers can "develop drugs that inhibit these enzymes without affecting the host''s metabolism" (lines 24941–24943). The observation of viral suppression with no host toxicity therefore points to an enzyme that belongs to the virus, not the cell. (Choice A) is correct: reverse transcriptase is a virus-encoded enzyme absent from uninfected host cells, so a drug that blocks it cripples viral replication while leaving normal host processes untouched—exactly the selectivity described. (Choice B) is incorrect: the RNA-to-DNA copying is not carried out by a host cell-cycle polymerase; if it were a host enzyme, blocking it would disrupt the patient''s own dividing cells and cause the very toxicity the experiment shows is absent. (Choice C) is incorrect: the order is reversed—reverse transcription produces the DNA copy first, and only that DNA is then integrated into the host genome (lines 24936–24938), so the copying step cannot depend on prior integration. (Choice D) is incorrect: uninfected host cells do not routinely reverse-transcribe their own mRNA into DNA; this copying is a virus-specific activity, which is precisely why it can be targeted without harming the host. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the fact that reverse transcriptase is a virus-supplied enzyme absent from normal cells to explain why inhibiting the RNA-to-DNA copying step selectively blocks the virus without host toxicity.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'f29988c8-09ce-46ae-87ec-2e2d75cd58c3';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'f29988c8-09ce-46ae-87ec-2e2d75cd58c3';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('f29988c8-09ce-46ae-87ec-2e2d75cd58c3', 'B', 'misconception', 'attributes a virus-specific enzymatic step to host machinery'),
  ('f29988c8-09ce-46ae-87ec-2e2d75cd58c3', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'swaps the order of reverse transcription and integration'),
  ('f29988c8-09ce-46ae-87ec-2e2d75cd58c3', 'D', 'misconception', 'believes host cells normally perform reverse transcription');

-- [Cytoskeleton: Microtubules] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A dividing cell is treated with a drug that binds polymerized microtubules and locks them in place, preventing them from disassembling into tubulin dimers, while still allowing new tubulin dimers to add on. Actin microfilaments, intermediate filaments, and all motor proteins remain fully functional. Which cellular event is MOST likely to fail as a direct result of this drug?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Movement of separated chromosomes toward the opposite poles of the cell"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Motor-driven sliding of membrane vesicles along an existing microtubule track"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Beating of cilia built from a stable 9 + 2 arrangement of microtubules"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Anchoring of the nucleus in place by a scaffold of intermediate filaments"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).'' Microtubules are hollow tubes built from α- and β-tubulin dimers that "can disassemble and reform quickly," and this dynamic behavior lets them "pull replicated chromosomes to opposite ends of a dividing cell." A drug that blocks depolymerization freezes the existing microtubules but does not destroy them, so processes that need microtubules to shorten fail, while processes that only need an intact track (or that do not use microtubules at all) continue. (Choice A) is correct: pulling separated chromosomes to the poles depends on the attached spindle microtubules shortening; because the drug prevents that disassembly, the microtubules cannot reel the chromosomes inward, so this movement is the event that fails. (Choice B) is incorrect: motor-driven vesicle transport rides along the surface of an intact microtubule, and the drug leaves that track in place—it may even stabilize it—so the motor protein can still walk its cargo along the tubule. (Choice C) is incorrect: the 9 + 2 microtubule core of a cilium is already a stable, assembled structure, so preventing depolymerization does not dismantle it, and ciliary beating is driven by motor proteins that remain intact. (Choice D) is incorrect: anchoring the nucleus is the job of intermediate filaments, a separate cytoskeletal element whose structural role is independent of tubulin, so a microtubule-specific drug does not disrupt it. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of microtubule dynamic instability to predict which process breaks when depolymerization—but not the track itself—is blocked.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '50ba0a8e-a7bc-4188-88fd-5b20bc648fe9';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '50ba0a8e-a7bc-4188-88fd-5b20bc648fe9';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('50ba0a8e-a7bc-4188-88fd-5b20bc648fe9', 'B', 'misconception', 'assumes any microtubule-targeting drug halts all microtubule-based transport'),
  ('50ba0a8e-a7bc-4188-88fd-5b20bc648fe9', 'C', 'misconception', 'assumes blocking depolymerization collapses stable microtubule structures like cilia'),
  ('50ba0a8e-a7bc-4188-88fd-5b20bc648fe9', 'D', 'misconception', 'attributes a microtubule drug''s effect to a tubulin-independent element (intermediate filaments)');

-- [Prokaryotic Cell Wall & Gram Staining] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher treats a rod-shaped bacterium with an enzyme that completely digests away its cell wall, leaving the plasma membrane and cytoplasm intact. The stripped cell is then transferred into distilled water, a solution far more dilute than the bacterial cytoplasm. Compared with an untreated bacterium placed in the same water, the stripped cell is most likely to:',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Burst, because water moves into the cell and the membrane can no longer be restrained from expanding"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Shrivel, because water moves out of the cell and the membrane collapses inward without support"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Remain unchanged, because removing the wall stops water from crossing the plasma membrane"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Swell only slightly, because the cytoplasm becomes hypotonic to the surrounding water"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).'' The bacterial cell wall is a rigid covering external to the plasma membrane that protects the cell and gives it shape; in bacteria and other walled organisms it "prevent[s] cell lysis in a hypotonic solution" because "the plasma membrane can only expand to the cell wall''s limit, so the cell will not lyse" (openstax-biology-2e.txt, lines 7152–7153). In distilled water, the cytoplasm is far more concentrated than the surroundings, so the solution is hypotonic to the cell and water moves in by osmosis. (Choice A) is correct: with the wall digested away, nothing limits how far the membrane can stretch, so the incoming water swells the cell until the membrane ruptures, just as a wall-less red blood cell "will burst, or lyse, when it swells beyond the plasma membrane''s capability to expand" (line 7143). (Choice B) is incorrect: shriveling (crenation/plasmolysis) occurs in a hypertonic environment where water leaves the cell; distilled water is hypotonic, so water enters rather than exits. (Choice C) is incorrect: the wall provides mechanical resistance, not a permeability barrier — water crosses the plasma membrane by osmosis whether or not the wall is present, so removing the wall does not stop water entry. (Choice D) is incorrect: it has the tonicity backward — the cytoplasm is hypertonic (not hypotonic) to distilled water, which is why water enters; and even granting that water enters, "only slightly" describes a walled cell, whose swelling is capped by the wall, whereas a wall-less cell has no expansion limit and bursts. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the wall''s mechanical role and the direction of osmosis to predict the fate of a wall-less cell in a hypotonic medium.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'ad450089-420f-4029-a72d-e0af94d5630f';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'ad450089-420f-4029-a72d-e0af94d5630f';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('ad450089-420f-4029-a72d-e0af94d5630f', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'confuses hypotonic (water in) with hypertonic (water out)'),
  ('ad450089-420f-4029-a72d-e0af94d5630f', 'C', 'misconception', 'treats the cell wall as an osmotic/permeability barrier rather than a mechanical restraint'),
  ('ad450089-420f-4029-a72d-e0af94d5630f', 'D', 'misconception', 'applies the walled-cell limited-swell outcome to a wall-less cell');

-- [Tissues: Connective Tissue] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher grows connective-tissue cells on a dish coated with extracellular matrix (ECM) proteins. When a specific matrix protein binds a receptor on the outer surface of a cell''s plasma membrane, the cell soon begins transcribing a new set of genes, even though the matrix protein itself never enters the cell. Which sequence best explains how binding at the cell surface leads to this change in gene transcription?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The bound receptor changes shape, altering nearby cytoskeletal microfilaments and triggering intracellular signals that reach the nucleus and switch specific genes on or off."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The matrix protein is carried through the receptor into the cytoplasm, where it travels to the nucleus and binds DNA to activate transcription directly."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The bound receptor opens a gap junction to a neighboring cell, and the transcription signal is passed between the two cells through that channel."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The matrix protein diffuses across the lipid bilayer on its own and then binds the receptor on the inner membrane surface to begin transcription."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).'' The extracellular matrix does more than hold cells together into tissues; it also lets cells sense their surroundings and change their behavior in response. The textbook explains that when a molecule in the matrix binds a receptor on the plasma membrane''s extracellular surface, "it changes the receptor''s molecular structure. The receptor, in turn, changes the microfilaments'' conformation positioned just inside the plasma membrane. These conformational changes induce chemical signals inside the cell that reach the nucleus and turn ''on'' or ''off'' the transcription of specific DNA sections, which affects the associated protein production." The key point is that the message is relayed inward by conformational change and intracellular signaling, not by the matrix protein physically entering the cell. (Choice A) is correct: the ligand-bound receptor changes shape, this remodels the cytoskeletal microfilaments just inside the membrane, and the resulting intracellular signals reach the nucleus to turn transcription on or off—exactly matching the described outcome in which the matrix protein never enters the cell. (Choice B) is incorrect: the stem specifies that the matrix protein never enters the cell, so it cannot be carried in to bind DNA directly; the receptor transmits the signal without importing the ligand. (Choice C) is incorrect: gap junctions pass ions and small molecules between adjacent cells, but the described response occurs within the cell that binds the matrix protein and involves the nucleus of that same cell, not transfer through a junction to a neighbor. (Choice D) is incorrect: the matrix protein is a large polar molecule that cannot slip through the hydrophobic lipid bilayer on its own, which is precisely why a surface receptor is needed to relay its signal inward. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the mechanism of extracellular-matrix signaling—surface binding, receptor conformational change, and intracellular relay to the nucleus—to a novel experiment rather than recalling a definition.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '64b14ea5-8a68-4f04-9967-0734f47a3250';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '64b14ea5-8a68-4f04-9967-0734f47a3250';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('64b14ea5-8a68-4f04-9967-0734f47a3250', 'B', 'misconception', 'ligand-must-enter-cell-to-affect-genes'),
  ('64b14ea5-8a68-4f04-9967-0734f47a3250', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'gap-junction-intercellular-transfer'),
  ('64b14ea5-8a68-4f04-9967-0734f47a3250', 'D', 'misconception', 'large-polar-molecule-crosses-bilayer-freely');

-- [Bacterial Shapes] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Two free-living bacterial species have cells of essentially identical cytoplasmic volume, but one is a coccus (roughly spherical) and the other is a bacillus (an elongated rod). Both live in a nutrient-poor pond where the rate at which each cell can acquire dissolved nutrients is limited by diffusion across its plasma membrane. Assuming the two cells have the same membrane transporter density, which cell is better positioned to sustain a higher per-cell nutrient uptake rate, and on what basis?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The bacillus, because elongating a fixed volume gives it a greater surface-area-to-volume ratio than the coccus."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The coccus, because a compact spherical form encloses the largest surface area for a given volume."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The bacillus, because its greater overall length gives it a larger internal volume in which to stockpile nutrients."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Neither, because uptake depends only on transporter density and is independent of the cell''s shape."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).'' Prokaryotes are classified by shape into cocci (spherical), bacilli (rod-shaped), and spirilli (spiral-shaped), and their small size and high surface-area-to-volume ratio let ions and organic molecules diffuse across the membrane quickly. For a fixed volume, a sphere has the smallest possible surface area, so the textbook notes that a cell can raise its surface area, and thus its diffusive exchange, by becoming "flat or thin and elongated" (openstax-biology-2e.txt, lines 5277-5285). When uptake is diffusion-limited across the membrane, more membrane surface per unit cytoplasm means a higher potential uptake rate. (Choice A) is correct: because a sphere minimizes surface area for a given volume, drawing that same volume out into a rod increases the membrane area available per unit of cytoplasm, raising the surface-area-to-volume ratio and supporting a higher diffusion-limited uptake rate at equal transporter density. (Choice B) is incorrect: this reverses the geometry — a sphere does not maximize but rather minimizes surface area for a fixed volume, giving the coccus the lowest surface-area-to-volume ratio of the two shapes (lines 5279-5281). (Choice C) is incorrect: the two cells were specified to have the same cytoplasmic volume, so no extra storage volume exists; moreover, when uptake is diffusion-limited the constraint is membrane surface area, not internal storage capacity. (Choice D) is incorrect: at equal transporter density the total uptake still scales with membrane surface area, and shape changes that area relative to volume, so morphology does affect the diffusion-limited uptake rate (lines 5281-5285). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the surface-area-to-volume principle to two bacterial morphologies and infer the functional consequence for diffusive nutrient uptake, rather than recall a shape name.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'ce101963-869e-42fc-a214-91840c341225';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'ce101963-869e-42fc-a214-91840c341225';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('ce101963-869e-42fc-a214-91840c341225', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'believes a sphere maximizes rather than minimizes surface area for a given volume'),
  ('ce101963-869e-42fc-a214-91840c341225', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'attributes an uptake advantage to internal storage volume despite equal volume and diffusion-limited uptake'),
  ('ce101963-869e-42fc-a214-91840c341225', 'D', 'misconception', 'believes membrane transport rate is set only by transporter density and is independent of cell shape');

-- [Tissues: Epithelial Tissue] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Inside the lung, oxygen and carbon dioxide must cross the epithelial lining of the alveoli by diffusion, and this exchange is most efficient when the barrier the gases traverse is as thin as possible. Which epithelial type is structurally best suited to line the alveolar surface where this rapid gas exchange occurs?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Simple squamous epithelium"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Simple cuboidal epithelium"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Simple columnar epithelium"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Transitional epithelium"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).'' Epithelia are classified by cell shape, and each shape suits a different physiological job: the rate of diffusion across a barrier increases as the barrier thins, so a surface specialized for gas exchange favors the flattest, thinnest cells. (Choice A) is correct: squamous epithelial cells are "round, flat," and when arranged in a single layer they "facilitate diffusion in tissues, such as the areas of gas exchange in the lungs and the exchange of nutrients and waste at blood capillaries" (lines 43273-43276); their flattened profile minimizes the distance \(O_2\) and \(CO_2\) must travel, which is exactly what alveolar gas exchange requires. (Choice B) is incorrect: cuboidal cells are "cube-shaped with a single, central nucleus" and line glandular tissue and "the walls of tubules and in the ducts of the kidney and liver," where they secrete and filter (lines 43298-43301); their boxy thickness is a longer diffusion path than a flat cell provides, so they suit secretion and reabsorption rather than gas exchange. (Choice C) is incorrect: columnar cells are "taller than they are wide" and "absorb material from the lumen of the digestive tract" (lines 43309-43312); their height maximizes absorptive machinery but also lengthens the diffusion distance, making them poorly suited to rapid gas transfer. (Choice D) is incorrect: transitional (uroepithelial) cells "appear only in the urinary system, primarily in the bladder and ureter" and are stratified so they can stretch as the organ fills (lines 43340-43342); although lungs expand and recoil, this stretch-tolerant, multilayered tissue is neither thin nor located in the alveoli. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between barrier thickness and diffusion rate to infer which epithelial structure serves a stated functional demand, rather than matching a described shape to its name.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'c0332653-3f8e-4a06-9690-ed621297a413';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'c0332653-3f8e-4a06-9690-ed621297a413';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('c0332653-3f8e-4a06-9690-ed621297a413', 'B', 'misconception', 'associates kidney/secretory cuboidal cells with any transport surface, ignoring that thickness impedes diffusion'),
  ('c0332653-3f8e-4a06-9690-ed621297a413', 'C', 'misconception', 'treats gas transfer as absorption and picks the absorptive columnar type'),
  ('c0332653-3f8e-4a06-9690-ed621297a413', 'D', 'misconception', 'reasons that because lungs expand and recoil they need stretch-tolerant transitional epithelium');

-- [Prokaryotic Growth & Metabolism] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A single bacterial species is inoculated into a flask of rich broth at a density of 500 cells/mL and incubated under conditions of unlimited nutrients. Under these conditions the population reproduces by binary fission with a generation time of 20 minutes, and this rate is maintained for the full incubation. If the culture is incubated for 2 hours, approximately how many cells/mL will be present at the end?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "1,000 cells/mL"}, {"label": "B", "text": "3,000 cells/mL"}, {"label": "C", "text": "32,000 cells/mL"}, {"label": "D", "text": "16,000 cells/mL"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).'' Prokaryotes reproduce asexually by binary fission, in which one cell replicates its single circular chromosome and pinches into two clones; when nutrients are unlimited the population grows exponentially, doubling once per generation time and tracing a J-shaped curve. The generation (doubling) time here is 20 minutes, so a 2-hour (120-minute) incubation spans \(120 \div 20 = 6\) generations, and each generation multiplies the population by 2, giving a final density of initial \(\times\, 2^{6}\). (Choice A) is incorrect: 1,000 cells/mL treats the population as doubling only once over the entire 2-hour incubation (\(500 \times 2\)), ignoring that each 20-minute generation is a separate doubling. (Choice B) is incorrect: 3,000 cells/mL comes from multiplying the starting count by the number of generations (\(500 \times 6\)), which models linear (additive) growth rather than the accelerating exponential growth that binary fission produces. (Choice C) is correct: over 120 minutes there are 6 generations, so the population is \(500 \times 2^{6} = 500 \times 64 = 32{,}000\) cells/mL. (Choice D) is incorrect: 16,000 cells/mL is \(500 \times 2^{5}\), an off-by-one error that counts only 5 doublings instead of the 6 that fit into 120 minutes. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the exponential-growth relationship for binary fission—converting incubation time into a number of generations and raising 2 to that power—rather than recall a definition.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '55fe879e-4c10-4e3d-a607-0f42be615279';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '55fe879e-4c10-4e3d-a607-0f42be615279';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('55fe879e-4c10-4e3d-a607-0f42be615279', 'A', 'misconception', 'single doubling over whole interval'),
  ('55fe879e-4c10-4e3d-a607-0f42be615279', 'B', 'misconception', 'linear/additive instead of exponential'),
  ('55fe879e-4c10-4e3d-a607-0f42be615279', 'D', 'misconception', 'off-by-one generation count');

-- [Tissues: Epithelial Tissue] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher is engineering an artificial membrane for the gas-exchange surface of a lung, where oxygen and carbon dioxide must cross the epithelial barrier as rapidly as possible between the air space and the underlying capillary blood. To minimize the distance gases must diffuse, which naturally occurring epithelium should the design be modeled on?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Simple squamous epithelium, as found lining the alveoli of the lungs"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Simple cuboidal epithelium, as found lining the tubules of the kidney"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Pseudostratified columnar epithelium, as found lining the trachea"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Stratified squamous epithelium, as found in the outer layer of the skin"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).'' Epithelia are classified by cell shape and the number of cell layers, and each arrangement suits a particular function: a single (simple) layer of flat (squamous) cells presents the thinnest possible barrier, whereas stacking cells into multiple (stratified) layers or building taller cell shapes increases barrier thickness at the cost of easy diffusion. The task is to match a functional demand — the shortest diffusion path — to the epithelial structure that provides it, rather than to identify a tissue from its name. (Choice A) is correct: simple squamous epithelium is a single layer of flat cells, and the text notes these cells "facilitate diffusion in tissues, such as the areas of gas exchange in the lungs"; because the cells are both thin (squamous) and one layer deep (simple), they minimize the distance gases must travel, which is exactly what the design requires. (Choice B) is incorrect: simple cuboidal cells are "cube-shaped with a single, central nucleus" and line kidney tubules and glands where they secrete and filter; their greater height makes a thicker barrier than flat squamous cells, so they are not optimized for rapid diffusion. (Choice C) is incorrect: pseudostratified columnar epithelium lines the respiratory tract''s conducting airways and uses cilia and goblet cells to move mucus, but its tall columnar cells form a comparatively thick barrier suited to protection and mucus transport, not gas exchange. (Choice D) is incorrect: stratified squamous epithelium consists of multiple layers and, as in the skin, provides "protection... from outside abrasion and damage"; the many layers deliberately lengthen the diffusion path, the opposite of what gas exchange needs. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between epithelial structure (cell shape and layer number) and function (diffusion distance) to select the tissue best suited to a novel design goal.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '031fda85-df68-441c-9382-17d53d88409e';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '031fda85-df68-441c-9382-17d53d88409e';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('031fda85-df68-441c-9382-17d53d88409e', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'correct-category-wrong-member'),
  ('031fda85-df68-441c-9382-17d53d88409e', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'right-location-wrong-function'),
  ('031fda85-df68-441c-9382-17d53d88409e', 'D', 'misconception', 'confuses-protection-with-exchange');

-- [Cytoskeleton: Centrioles and the Centrosome] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A cell biologist treats dividing animal cells with a drug that binds free tubulin and prevents it from polymerizing, while leaving actin filaments intact. Which structure or event in these cells will be MOST directly disrupted by the drug?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Assembly of the centrioles and the mitotic spindle that segregates the chromosomes"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Formation of the cleavage furrow that constricts the cell during cytokinesis"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Replication of the DNA that produces the duplicated sister chromatids"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Breakdown of the nuclear envelope that releases the chromosomes into the cytoplasm"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).'' Centrioles are cylinders built from microtubules, and the mitotic spindle is likewise assembled from microtubules that grow from microtubule-organizing centers; the textbook notes that microtubules "pull replicated chromosomes to opposite ends of a dividing cell." A drug that blocks tubulin polymerization therefore prevents any microtubule-based structure from forming, while actin-based and other structures are unaffected. (Choice A) is correct: both centrioles (nine triplets of microtubules) and the spindle fibers are microtubule structures, so preventing tubulin from polymerizing directly blocks their assembly and the chromosome segregation that depends on the spindle. (Choice B) is incorrect: the cleavage furrow of animal-cell cytokinesis is produced by a contractile ring of actin (microfilaments), not microtubules, so it is not the structure most directly disrupted by a tubulin-specific drug. (Choice C) is incorrect: DNA replication is carried out by DNA polymerase and its associated enzymes during S phase and does not require microtubules, so the drug does not directly block production of sister chromatids. (Choice D) is incorrect: nuclear envelope breakdown is driven by phosphorylation of nuclear lamins and vesiculation of the envelope, an event that does not depend on tubulin polymerization. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the fact that centrioles and the spindle are built from microtubules to predict which cellular event a tubulin-specific inhibitor will disrupt.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '2be8902c-0ae9-49f7-a453-b0587bd0e491';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '2be8902c-0ae9-49f7-a453-b0587bd0e491';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('2be8902c-0ae9-49f7-a453-b0587bd0e491', 'B', 'misconception', 'conflates microtubule- and actin-based structures'),
  ('2be8902c-0ae9-49f7-a453-b0587bd0e491', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'real division event that does not require microtubules'),
  ('2be8902c-0ae9-49f7-a453-b0587bd0e491', 'D', 'misconception', 'attributes a non-microtubule event to microtubules');

-- [Prokaryotic Domains: Archaea and Bacteria] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A microbiologist synthesizes a novel antibiotic that kills cells by blocking the cross-linking of peptidoglycan strands, so that the target cell cannot assemble a functional cell wall. She applies the drug at high concentration to pure cultures representing each of the three domains of life. Assuming every culture is otherwise viable, the cells of which domain are the ONLY ones this drug can kill?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Bacteria"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Archaea"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Eukarya"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Both Archaea and Eukarya"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).'' The drug''s target is peptidoglycan, the polysaccharide polymer whose strands are cross-linked by short peptides to form a rigid mesh. Of the three domains of life, only Bacteria build their cell wall from peptidoglycan, so only bacterial cells depend on the very cross-linking step this drug blocks. (Choice A) is correct: bacterial cell walls are composed largely of peptidoglycan, and interfering with its cross-linking prevents the wall from forming, which is exactly how many real cell-wall antibiotics kill bacteria; Bacteria are therefore the only domain this drug can kill. (Choice B) is incorrect: Archaea are prokaryotes, but their cell walls contain no peptidoglycan, being built instead from pseudopeptidoglycan, polysaccharides, glycoproteins, or pure protein, so a peptidoglycan-cross-linking inhibitor has no target in an archaean and cannot kill it. (Choice C) is incorrect: eukaryotic cells never make peptidoglycan; those eukaryotes that have walls (plants, fungi, many protists) use cellulose or chitin, and animal cells have no wall at all, so this drug has nothing to disrupt in Eukarya. (Choice D) is incorrect: it correctly recognizes that neither Archaea nor Eukarya makes peptidoglycan, but that is precisely why the drug cannot kill either of them; the domain it CAN kill is the peptidoglycan-dependent one, Bacteria. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the fact that peptidoglycan is the defining cell-wall component of Bacteria alone to predict which of the three domains a peptidoglycan-targeting drug would actually kill.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '70fa62e2-f09c-47b8-b145-ba056ba81be6';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '70fa62e2-f09c-47b8-b145-ba056ba81be6';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('70fa62e2-f09c-47b8-b145-ba056ba81be6', 'B', 'misconception', 'assumes the other prokaryotic domain shares peptidoglycan-based walls with Bacteria'),
  ('70fa62e2-f09c-47b8-b145-ba056ba81be6', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'knows eukaryotes can have cell walls but forgets those walls are not peptidoglycan'),
  ('70fa62e2-f09c-47b8-b145-ba056ba81be6', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'identifies the two peptidoglycan-free domains but labels them as killed instead of spared');

-- [Prokaryotic Cell Wall & Gram Staining] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A rod-shaped bacterium is growing in a broth that is isotonic to its cytoplasm. A researcher adds a compound that prevents the cell from building and maintaining its cell wall, and then dilutes the culture with a large volume of distilled water so the surrounding medium becomes strongly hypotonic to the cytoplasm. Based on the normal role of the bacterial cell wall, what is the MOST likely fate of these cells?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The cells swell and burst, because the plasma membrane expands beyond its limit as water enters the cytoplasm"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The cells shrink and crenate, because water leaves the cytoplasm and the membrane pulls inward from the surface"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The cells stay intact and unchanged, because the plasma membrane alone blocks the net entry of water into the cell"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The cells stay intact and rigid, because leftover wall material still resists the incoming water and limits swelling"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).'' The bacterial cell wall surrounds the plasma membrane and acts "as an extra layer of protection, helps the cell maintain its shape, and prevents dehydration"; critically, it also limits how far the membrane can stretch. The textbook notes that organisms "such as plants, fungi, bacteria, and some protists, have cell walls that surround the plasma membrane and prevent cell lysis in a hypotonic solution," because "the plasma membrane can only expand to the cell wall''s limit, so the cell will not lyse." When the wall is removed or cannot be maintained, that protective limit is gone. (Choice A) is correct: in a hypotonic medium water moves down its concentration gradient into the cytoplasm, and with no rigid wall to cap the membrane''s expansion, the membrane stretches until "the spaces between the lipids and proteins become too large" and the cell bursts, or lyses, just as a wall-less red blood cell does in a hypotonic solution. (Choice B) is incorrect because shrinking and crenation occur when a cell loses water in a hypertonic environment; here the medium is hypotonic, so the net water movement is inward, not outward. (Choice C) is incorrect because the plasma membrane is semipermeable and freely allows osmotic water flow; it cannot by itself stop net water entry, which is precisely why wall-less cells lyse in hypotonic conditions. (Choice D) is incorrect because the compound blocks the cell from building and maintaining an intact wall, so there is no continuous rigid structure to cap membrane expansion; scattered leftover material cannot provide the mechanical limit that an intact wall does. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the cell wall''s role in limiting membrane expansion to a novel hypotonic scenario and chain osmosis to the predicted outcome.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'adeb9b6d-c873-4797-8dcd-3ecee738c7a9';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'adeb9b6d-c873-4797-8dcd-3ecee738c7a9';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('adeb9b6d-c873-4797-8dcd-3ecee738c7a9', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'confuses hypotonic (water in, swelling) with hypertonic (water out, shrinking)'),
  ('adeb9b6d-c873-4797-8dcd-3ecee738c7a9', 'C', 'misconception', 'believes the plasma membrane alone can prevent osmotic water entry'),
  ('adeb9b6d-c873-4797-8dcd-3ecee738c7a9', 'D', 'misconception', 'assumes any residual wall material still confers full osmotic protection');

-- [Prokaryotic Cell Wall & Gram Staining] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher grows a rod-shaped soil bacterium and treats one sample with a compound that blocks assembly of the bacterial cell wall while leaving the plasma membrane intact. When the treated cells are placed in a dilute (hypotonic) medium, they swell and burst, whereas untreated cells retain their rod shape. Which statement best explains why losing the cell wall causes the cells to rupture in this medium?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Without the rigid wall to resist the inward flow of water, the plasma membrane expands past the limit it can withstand as water enters the cells, so the cells lyse"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Without the wall, the cells can no longer pump ions out across their surface, so internal solutes accumulate and draw in water until the cells lyse"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Without the wall, water is instead driven out of the cells, and the shrinking cytoplasm pulls on and ruptures the plasma membrane"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Without the wall, the cells lose the hydrophobic barrier that normally blocks water entry, so water floods in unopposed and the cells lyse"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).'' Most bacteria have a cell wall that, in the words of the text, "acts as an extra layer of protection, helps the cell maintain its shape, and prevents dehydration" (openstax-biology-2e.txt, lines 5226-5227). In a hypotonic environment, water moves into the cell down its concentration gradient; bacteria "have cell walls that surround the plasma membrane and prevent cell lysis in a hypotonic solution," because "the plasma membrane can only expand to the cell wall''s limit, so the cell will not lyse" (lines 7152-7153). Removing the wall removes that mechanical brake. (Choice A) is correct: in the hypotonic medium water enters the cell, and without the rigid wall to cap how far the plasma membrane can stretch, the membrane expands beyond its limit and the cell bursts, exactly as the text describes for cells that lack this wall constraint. (Choice B) is incorrect: the cell wall is a passive, rigid structural covering, not an ion pump; solute transport is a function of the plasma membrane, so losing the wall does not disable ion export, and this misattributes an active transport role to a mechanical structure. (Choice C) is incorrect: it reverses the direction of osmosis for a hypotonic medium—water enters (not leaves) a cell surrounded by more dilute fluid; water loss and cytoplasmic shrinkage occur in a hypertonic medium (plasmolysis), which is the opposite of the scenario described. (Choice D) is incorrect: the barrier to free water movement and selective permeability is the plasma membrane, not the cell wall; the wall is porous to water and small solutes, so its loss does not expose the cytoplasm to water that was previously blocked. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the wall''s mechanical, osmosis-resisting function and the direction of water flow in a hypotonic medium to predict why wall-less cells lyse.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '3480ea63-698a-4a63-a1af-7f377d0401db';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '3480ea63-698a-4a63-a1af-7f377d0401db';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('3480ea63-698a-4a63-a1af-7f377d0401db', 'B', 'misconception', 'wall-as-active-transporter'),
  ('3480ea63-698a-4a63-a1af-7f377d0401db', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'hypotonic-hypertonic-swap'),
  ('3480ea63-698a-4a63-a1af-7f377d0401db', 'D', 'misconception', 'wall-as-permeability-barrier');

-- [Cytoskeleton: Microtubules] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A cell biologist tracks a membrane-bound vesicle moving in a straight, directed path across the interior of a cell. Electron microscopy shows the vesicle riding along a single cytoskeletal fiber with a diameter of about 25 nm. Which fiber is the vesicle traveling along, and what is that fiber''s characteristic composition?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "A microtubule, built from polymerized \\(\\alpha\\)-tubulin/\\(\\beta\\)-tubulin dimers"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A microfilament, built from two intertwined strands of actin"}, {"label": "C", "text": "An intermediate filament, built from several wound strands of fibrous proteins"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A microtubule, built from two intertwined strands of actin"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).'' The three cytoskeletal fibers differ in both diameter and function: microfilaments (~7 nm) are intertwined actin strands that drive movement and contractility, intermediate filaments (~8–10 nm) are wound fibrous-protein strands that bear tension, and microtubules (~25 nm) are hollow tubes of \(\alpha\)-tubulin/\(\beta\)-tubulin dimers that resist compression and serve as tracks along which vesicles move through the cell. Two clues must agree: the ~25 nm diameter identifies the widest fiber, and directed vesicle transport is the job assigned specifically to that fiber. (Choice A) is correct: only microtubules are ~25 nm (the widest fiber) and act as the tracks for directed vesicle transport, and they are composed of polymerized \(\alpha\)-tubulin/\(\beta\)-tubulin dimers—both the size and the transport role match. (Choice B) is incorrect: microfilaments are the narrowest fibers (~7 nm) of intertwined actin and function in cellular movement and contractility, not as the ~25 nm transport highways described, so the diameter rules them out. (Choice C) is incorrect: intermediate filaments (~8–10 nm) are wound fibrous-protein strands whose role is purely structural—bearing tension and anchoring organelles—not carrying vesicles, and they are far narrower than 25 nm. (Choice D) is incorrect: it correctly labels the fiber a microtubule but assigns it the actin composition of a microfilament; microtubules are made of tubulin dimers, not intertwined actin strands. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the measured diameter and the observed transport behavior together to identify the fiber and infer its correct composition, rather than recalling a single fact.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '754cd5b2-10c7-4619-9ae2-8e12ec611209';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '754cd5b2-10c7-4619-9ae2-8e12ec611209';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('754cd5b2-10c7-4619-9ae2-8e12ec611209', 'B', 'misconception', 'confuses fiber diameter/type — attributes transport role to actin microfilaments'),
  ('754cd5b2-10c7-4619-9ae2-8e12ec611209', 'C', 'misconception', 'confuses fiber function — assigns transport to tension-bearing intermediate filaments'),
  ('754cd5b2-10c7-4619-9ae2-8e12ec611209', 'D', 'misconception', 'correct fiber label paired with wrong (actin) composition');

COMMIT;
